No panic out of the message pump's callbacks.

Applies to: tauri-runtime-cef 3.0.0-alpha.7 (crates.io) + 0001, 0003, 0004, applied in
vendor/tauri-runtime-cef. Not offered upstream.

Why: the pump's NSTimer target (`scheduleWork:`, `timerTimeout:`) is called from AppKit and
the browser process handler (the pump's `OnScheduleMessagePumpWork`, and the three other
callbacks of that handler) from CEF's C code; a panic unwinding out of one aborts the app
with every agent in it. Each is caught and logged: the pump clears its lock's poison and
starts over from its 33 ms timer, the handler answers CEF as if it had done nothing
(`on_already_running_app_relaunch`: handled, as it answers today for a link it does not
know). A panic inside a callback CEF makes from `CefDoMessageLoopWork` still aborts in
CEF's own frame before it reaches the timer: those callbacks are guarded where they reach
the app (`eos-shell/src/catch.rs`).

Not here: a slower pump while the window is hidden or covered. Tried with a 250 ms
safety-net timer while the app was inactive and no window visible: requests through the
in-process scheme handler then waited up to a timer's length (`responsiveness.py`: whole
trips of 240-285 ms, frame gaps of 300-570 ms while covered), so CEF relies on the timer
for some of its work and the 33 ms cap stays.

